.Dd November 6, 2026
.Dt FILEOPS 9
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fileops ,
.Nm fo_pathconf ,
.Nm fo_statfs ,
.Nm fo_fsync ,
.Nm fo_setattr ,
.Nm fo_fadvise ,
.Nm fo_readdir ,
.Nm fo_copy_file_range ,
.Nm fo_acl ,
.Nm fo_extattr ,
.Nm fo_fcntl ,
.Nm fo_advlock
.Nd operations on an open file descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/param.h
.In sys/file.h
.Ft int
.Fn fo_pathconf "struct file *fp" "int name" "long *valuep" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_statfs "struct file *fp" "struct statfs *buf" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_fsync "struct file *fp" "int mode" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_setattr "struct file *fp" "struct vattr *vap" "struct ucred *active_cred" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_fadvise "struct file *fp" "off_t offset" "off_t len" "int advice" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_readdir "struct file *fp" "struct uio *uio" "off_t *basep" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_copy_file_range "struct file *infp" "off_t *inoffp" "struct file *outfp" "off_t *outoffp" "size_t *lenp" "unsigned int flags" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_acl "struct file *fp" "int op" "int type" "struct acl *aclp" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_extattr "struct file *fp" "int op" "int attrnamespace" "const char *attrname" "void *data" "size_t nbytes" "struct uio *auio" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_fcntl "struct file *fp" "int cmd" "intptr_t arg" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_advlock "struct file *fp" "void *id" "int op" "struct flock *fl" "int flags" "struct thread *td"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Every open file
.Pq Vt "struct file"
carries a pointer,
.Va f_ops ,
to a
.Vt "struct fileops"
table that implements each operation a process can perform on the
descriptor.
System calls that take a file descriptor dispatch through this table and
never through the descriptor's type
.Pq Va f_type
or through a vnode the file may carry
.Pq Va f_vnode .
A descriptor type therefore answers for itself, and a type that is not
backed by a vnode is complete without one.
.Pp
Each member has an inline dispatch function of the same name.
Members that a table leaves
.Dv NULL
dispatch to a type-neutral default, named
.Fn invfo_* ,
which returns the error the operation has always returned for a descriptor
of no particular type.
Vnode-backed descriptors use the
.Fn vn_*
implementations.
.Pp
A table also describes its type through
.Va fo_flags .
Besides
.Dv DFLAG_PASSABLE
and
.Dv DFLAG_SEEKABLE ,
.Dv DFLAG_PRIVWRITE
marks a type whose
.Dv MAP_PRIVATE
mappings are copy-on-write and never reach the object, so
.Xr mmap 2
grants
.Dv PROT_WRITE
on such a mapping of a descriptor that lacks the
.Dv CAP_MMAP_W
right.
Vnode files and
.Xr devfs 4
nodes set it;
.Xr mmap 2
tests the flag, never the descriptor's type.
.Ss Descriptors that carry a vnode without being vnode files
Several descriptor types keep a reference to the vnode they were opened
through although they are not
.Dv DTYPE_VNODE
files served by
.Va vnops :
FIFOs
.Pq Va pipeops ,
pseudo-terminal masters,
.Xr devfs 4
nodes under their own tables, and linuxkpi files.
For every operation that is answered by the vnode, these tables install the
.Fn vn_*
implementation explicitly, so the descriptor answers exactly as its vnode
does; no default inspects
.Va f_vnode .
A table that does not carry a vnode must not install a
.Fn vn_*
member.
.Pp
Two tables carry a vnode only on some creation paths: a pseudo-terminal
master has one when opened through
.Pa /dev/ptmx
and none from
.Xr posix_openpt 2 ;
a linuxkpi file has one when opened through
.Xr devfs 4
and none from
.Fn fd_install .
Those tables install the
.Fn vn_*_opt
variants, which answer through the vnode when it is present and give the
type-neutral answer otherwise.
Installing a plain
.Fn vn_*
member there dereferences a null vnode.
.Ss fo_pathconf
.Fn fo_pathconf
answers
.Xr fpathconf 2 :
it stores the value of the configuration variable
.Fa name
through
.Fa valuep
and returns 0, or returns an error.
.Dv _PC_ASYNC_IO
is answered by the system call for every descriptor and never reaches the
table.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_pathconf
locks the vnode shared and calls
.Xr VOP_PATHCONF 9 .
Directories, regular files,
.Dv O_PATH
files, FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters, devfs nodes and linuxkpi files all
use it.
.It pipes and sockets
Answer
.Dv _PC_PIPE_BUF
with
.Dv PIPE_BUF
and return
.Er EINVAL
for every other name.
A FIFO is a pipe that carries a vnode and answers through the vnode
instead.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_pathconf
returns
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.El
.Ss fo_statfs
.Fn fo_statfs
answers
.Xr fstatfs 2
by filling
.Fa buf .
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_statfs
references the vnode's mount, runs the MAC mount-stat check, and calls
.Fn kern_do_statfs ,
which busies the mount, calls
.Xr VFS_STATFS 9
and applies jail
.Xr statfs 2
enforcement.
All of it needs the mount, so none of it is left in the system call.
Directories, regular files,
.Dv O_PATH
files, FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters, devfs nodes and linuxkpi files all
use it.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_statfs
returns
.Er EINVAL ,
the error a descriptor without a vnode has always received.
.El
.Ss fo_fsync
.Fn fo_fsync
flushes the file according to
.Fa mode :
.Bl -tag -width "FO_FSYNC_FULL" -compact
.It Dv FO_FSYNC_FULL
data and metadata, as
.Xr fsync 2
and
.Dv LIO_SYNC ;
.It Dv FO_FSYNC_DATA
data only, as
.Xr fdatasync 2
and
.Dv LIO_DSYNC ;
.It Dv FO_FSYNC_FS
the whole filesystem the file is on, as Linux
.Fn syncfs .
.El
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_fsync
is the one implementation for the system calls,
.Xr aio_fsync 2
and
.Fn syncfs .
It suspends writes on the mount, locks the vnode, cleans its pages and
calls
.Xr VOP_FSYNC 9
or
.Xr VOP_FDATASYNC 9 ,
retrying on
.Er ERELOOKUP ;
for
.Dv FO_FSYNC_FS
it busies the mount and runs
.Xr VFS_SYNC 9 .
It enters the file context itself, so every table installs it directly.
Directories, regular files, FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters, devfs nodes,
linuxkpi files and
.Dv O_PATH
files use it;
.Xr fsync 2
and
.Xr fdatasync 2
refuse an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor with
.Er EBADF
before reaching the table, as they always did, while
.Xr aio_fsync 2
and
.Fn syncfs
accept one, as they always did.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_fsync
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss fo_setattr
.Fn fo_setattr
sets the access, modification and birth times and the file flags given in
.Fa vap ;
a field holding
.Dv VNOVAL
is left alone.
Mode, owner and size have their own operations
.Pq Fn fo_chmod , Fn fo_chown , Fn fo_truncate
and are refused with
.Er EINVAL .
It answers
.Xr futimens 2 ,
.Xr futimes 2 ,
.Xr fchflags 2 ,
Linux file attribute flags, and attribute changes made through
.Pa /dev/fd/N ,
which
.Xr fdescfs 4
splits across the four operations.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_setattr
carries the checks the system calls used to make themselves: the vnode
audit record, the privilege to set flags on a device, the MAC flag and time
checks and notifications, and the birth-time adjustment of
.Xr utimes 2 .
It then suspends writes on the mount, locks the vnode and calls
.Xr VOP_SETATTR 9 .
Directories, regular files, FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters, devfs nodes,
linuxkpi files and
.Dv O_PATH
files use it;
.Xr futimens 2 ,
.Xr futimes 2
and
.Xr fchflags 2
refuse an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor with
.Er EBADF
before reaching the table, as they always did.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_setattr
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss fo_fadvise
.Fn fo_fadvise
applies a
.Xr posix_fadvise 2
hint to the byte range
.Fa offset
and
.Fa len .
The system call validates the range and the advice, records the audit
argument, and refuses a descriptor without
.Dv DFLAG_SEEKABLE
with
.Er ESPIPE
before the table is consulted.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_fadvise
keeps the sequential, random and no-reuse regions in the file's
.Va f_advice ,
which
.Fn vn_read
and
.Fn vn_write
consult, and passes the will-need and do-not-need hints to
.Xr VOP_ADVISE 9 .
Only a regular file has anything to advise about; directories and devices
return
.Er ENODEV .
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_fadvise
returns
.Er ENODEV .
.El
.Ss fo_readdir
.Fn fo_readdir
answers
.Xr getdirentries 2 :
it reads directory entries into
.Fa uio
from
.Va uio_offset ,
leaves
.Va uio_offset
at the next entry, and stores the offset the read began from through
.Fa basep .
The system call holds the file's offset lock around the call, checks
.Dv FREAD ,
and refuses
.Dv O_PATH
descriptors with
.Er EBADF ,
as it always did.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_readdir
locks the vnode shared, returns
.Er ENOTDIR
for anything that is not a directory, runs the MAC readdir check and
.Xr VOP_READDIR 9 ,
and, when a union mount's root directory is exhausted, moves the file to
the covered directory and reads on, as it always did.
Directories use it; FIFOs, devfs nodes and vnode-carrying pseudo-terminal
masters and linuxkpi files reach it and get
.Er ENOTDIR .
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_readdir
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss fo_copy_file_range
.Fn fo_copy_file_range
answers
.Xr copy_file_range 2 ,
dispatched on the input file's table.
.Fa lenp
carries the requested length in and the copied length out; the offsets
are updated whatever the result.
The system call validates the flags, clamps the length, takes both files'
offset locks, checks the open modes and refuses negative offsets; it
consults the table only when both descriptors install the operation, and
returns
.Er EINVAL
otherwise, which is what descriptors without a vnode received.
.Bl -tag -width "vnode files"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_copy_range
refuses overlapping ranges of one file, range-locks both vnodes, and runs
.Fn vn_copy_file_range
inside the file context.
Only
.Va vnops
installs it: FIFOs, devices and pseudo-terminal masters were refused by
.Fn vn_copy_file_range
and keep the same
.Er EINVAL
through the default.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_copy_file_range
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss fo_acl
.Fn fo_acl
answers the
.Fn __acl_get_fd ,
.Fn __acl_set_fd ,
.Fn __acl_delete_fd
and
.Fn __acl_aclcheck_fd
system calls, selected by
.Fa op
.Pq Dv FO_ACL_GET , Dv FO_ACL_SET , Dv FO_ACL_DELETE , Dv FO_ACL_CHECK ,
for the
.Vt acl_type_t
.Fa type .
The system calls record the descriptor audit argument and refuse set and
delete on an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor with
.Er EBADF ,
as they always did; get and check accept one.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_acl
calls the
.Fn vacl_*
helper for the operation, each of which locks the vnode and runs its own
MAC check and audit record before
.Xr VOP_GETACL 9 ,
.Xr VOP_SETACL 9
or
.Xr VOP_ACLCHECK 9 .
Regular files, directories,
.Dv O_PATH
files, FIFOs, devfs nodes and vnode-carrying pseudo-terminal masters and
linuxkpi files use it, and get whatever their filesystem answers.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_acl
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss fo_extattr
.Fn fo_extattr
answers the
.Xr extattr_set_fd 2 ,
.Xr extattr_get_fd 2 ,
.Xr extattr_delete_fd 2
and
.Xr extattr_list_fd 2
system calls and the Linux
.Fn f*xattr
family, selected by
.Fa op
.Pq Dv FO_EXTATTR_SET , Dv FO_EXTATTR_GET , Dv FO_EXTATTR_DELETE , Dv FO_EXTATTR_LIST .
Set and get take the user buffer in
.Fa data
and
.Fa nbytes
.Pq get with a null buffer returns the size ,
list fills
.Fa auio ,
delete takes neither.
The system calls record the audit arguments and accept
.Dv O_PATH
descriptors, as they always did.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_extattr
calls the
.Fn extattr_*_vp
helper for the operation, each of which locks the vnode and runs its own
MAC check before
.Xr VOP_SETEXTATTR 9 ,
.Xr VOP_GETEXTATTR 9 ,
.Fn VOP_DELETEEXTATTR
or
.Xr VOP_LISTEXTATTR 9 .
Regular files, directories,
.Dv O_PATH
files, FIFOs, devfs nodes and vnode-carrying pseudo-terminal masters and
linuxkpi files use it, and get whatever their filesystem answers.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_extattr
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss fo_fcntl
.Fn fo_fcntl
answers the
.Xr fcntl 2
commands whose meaning depends on the descriptor type and that the generic
file layer cannot answer itself:
.Dv F_READAHEAD
.Po
.Dv F_RDAHEAD
arrives as
.Dv F_READAHEAD
with the byte count already in
.Fa arg
.Pc
and
.Dv F_ISUNIONSTACK
from
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
and the kernel-private
.Dv FO_FCNTL_GET_RW_HINT
and
.Dv FO_FCNTL_SET_RW_HINT
behind the Linux read/write life-time hints.
A command that yields a value stores it in
.Va td_retval[0] ,
as
.Fn kern_fcntl
does.
.Xr fcntl 2
refuses read-ahead on an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor with
.Er EBADF
before the table is consulted, as it always did.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_fcntl
sets the file's sequential read-ahead on a regular file
.Pq Er ENOTTY otherwise ,
reports whether the mount is a union stack, and reads or sets the vnode's
write hint after a
.Dv VADMIN
access check.
Regular files, directories,
.Dv O_PATH
files and devfs nodes use it.
FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters and linuxkpi files do not: the type tests
these commands used to make returned
.Er EBADF
for them, and the default keeps doing so.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_fcntl
returns
.Er EBADF .
.El
.Ss fo_advlock
.Fn fo_advlock
answers
.Xr flock 2
.Pq Dv F_FLOCK in Fa flags , the file as Fa id ,
the
.Xr fcntl 2
record locks
.Po
.Dv F_POSIX
with the process leader as
.Fa id ,
.Dv F_OFD
with the file
.Pc
and the unlock of all POSIX locks that every
.Xr close 2
owes the process, with
.Fa op
one of
.Dv F_SETLK , F_GETLK , F_UNLCK
or
.Dv F_UNLCKSYS .
.Xr fcntl 2
rejects a lock request for a mode the descriptor was not opened with before
the table is consulted; whether the table takes locks at all is the table's
business.
The close-time unlock skips a table that has no
.Fn fo_advlock
member, and
.Xr fcntl 2
refuses such a descriptor with
.Er EBADF
before it looks at the lock argument, as the type test did;
.Xr flock 2
lets the default answer.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_advlock
checks, for
.Dv F_OFD ,
that the filesystem declares
.Dv VFCF_OFDLOCKS
.Pq Er EOPNOTSUPP otherwise
and calls
.Xr VOP_ADVLOCK 9
inside the file context.
Regular files, directories and devfs nodes use it.
A FIFO installs
.Fn pipe_advlock ,
which takes
.Dv F_FLOCK
requests to the vnode and refuses the rest as a pipe does.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_advlock
returns what the type tests gave a descriptor that takes no locks:
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
to
.Xr flock 2
.Pq Dv F_FLOCK ,
.Er EBADF
to the
.Xr fcntl 2
record locks.
.Dv O_PATH
files, pseudo-terminal masters and linuxkpi files do not install the member:
the type tests these system calls used to make refused them, and they stay
refused.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Each operation returns 0 on success or an
.Xr errno 2
value.
